## DESCRIPTION
## Trigonometric Functions

## DBsubject(Trigonometry)
## DBchapter(Triangle trigonometry)
## DBsection(Applications of special triangles & right triangles)
## Date(01/01/10)
## Institution(Fort Lewis College)
## Author(Paul Pearson)
## Level(5)
## MO(1)
## TitleText1('Functions Modeling Change')
## TitleText2('Functions Modeling Change')
## TitleText3('Functions Modeling Change')
## AuthorText1('Connally')
## AuthorText2('Connally')
## AuthorText3('Connally')
## EditionText1('3')
## EditionText2('4')
## EditionText3('5')
## Section1(6.Tools)
## Section2('7.4')
## Section3('8.1')
## Problem1(23)
## Problem2('25')
## Problem3('27')
## KEYWORDS('trig')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros(
    'PGstandard.pl',            'PGML.pl',
    'plots.pl',                 'contextTrigDegrees.pl',
    'parserNumberWithUnits.pl', 'PGcourse.pl'
);

Context('TrigDegrees');

$y = random(100, 300, 50);

$ansz = NumberWithUnits("2*$y/sqrt(3)", "ft");
$ansx = NumberWithUnits("$y/sqrt(3)",   "ft");

BEGIN_PGML
The top of a [` [$y] `] foot vertical tower is to be
anchored by cables that make an angle of [` 60^{\circ} `]
with the ground.  Please include units in your answers.
All trig functions will be evaluated in degrees.

a) How long must each cable be? [_]{$ansz}

b) How far from the base of the tower should anchors be placed? [_]{$ansx}

[@  helpLink('units')  @]*
END_PGML

$twoy = 2 * $y;

$plot = Plot(
    xmin            => -2,
    xmax            =>  5,
    ymin            => -1,
    ymax            => 2.5,
    xvisible        =>  0,
    yvisible        =>  0,
    show_grid       =>  0,
    rounded_corners =>  0,
    aspect_ratio    => 1
);
$plot->add_function(
    [ '32*(x-0.25)^2', 'x',  0,    0.25, color => 'blue' ],
    [ '32*(x+0.65)^2', 'x', -0.65, -0.4, color => 'blue' ]
);
$plot->add_dataset(
    [ [ -0.4, 2 ], [ 0, 2 ], color => 'blue' ],
    [ [ -2,   0 ], [ 5, 0 ] ],
    [ [ 0,    0 ], [ 0, 2 ], [ 4.5, 0 ], [ 0, 0 ], width => 1 ]
);

$plot->add_label(
    [  2,    0,    '\\(x\\)', v_align => 'top' ],
    [  2.25, 1.25, '\\(h\\)' ],
    [ -0.85, 1,    '250' ]
);

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION



The figure below illustrates this situation.
>> [!Figure showing a tower with a wire extending from the top out to the ground.!]{$plot}{300} <<

We have a right triangle with legs [`x`] and [`[$y]`] and hypotenuse [`h`].
Thus,
[```\begin{aligned}
\sin60^{\circ} &= \frac{[$y]}{h}\\
h &= \frac{[$y]}{\sin60^{\circ}} = \frac{[$y]}{\sqrt3/2} = \frac{[$twoy]}{\sqrt3}
\,\mbox{feet}.
\end{aligned}
```]
To find the distance [`x`], we can relate the angle and its opposite and
adjacent legs by writing
[```\begin{aligned}
\tan60^{\circ} &= \frac{[$y]}{x}\\
x &= \frac{[$y]}{\tan60^{\circ}} = \frac{[$y]}{\sqrt{3}}\,\mbox{feet}.
\end{aligned}
```]
We could also write the equation [`x^2+[$y]^2=h^2`] and
substitute [`h=\frac{[$twoy]}{\sqrt3}`] ft to solve for [`x`].

END_PGML_SOLUTION

COMMENT(
    "Redefines basic trig functions to be in degrees (instead of the default radians)"
);

ENDDOCUMENT();
